Z||000000||JetroGT||04-05-2000||04:39 AM||||<BR>I need to know if it is possible to replace the engine and trans mounts with the engine still in the car. If so, how would I go about doing it the right way? Jack stands and a cherry picker?<BR> This is on my 87 GT 5 spd. The previous owner, or owners let the dog bone go bad causing the mounts to go bad also. <BR>Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||BroCar 87||04-05-2000||01:23 PM||||hey hey hey..... I have the same problem. I have a 87 coupe (4 cyl.), I thought about just replacing the mounts and dog bone... but that would be too easy. haha. I have decided to save that money for an upgrade to a v-6. I will relace them when I put in the craddle assembly. I just don't like the 4 cylinder in this car... whats the phrase "unjustified". lol. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||04-05-2000||02:25 PM||||not hard. You need tht dog bone out when you do it. ((Except 86.5+ 4cyl w/ automatic. Leave the dog bone in for those. It's the only thing keeping the motor from falling over.))<P>unbolt them, jack the motor up a little and that's about it. leave the front ones loose until the back ones are in if you are doing them to. Sometimes the new ones need that smidge of extra play to line up.<P>if you put the jack under the oil pan make sure you use a foot long chunk of 2x4 or better yet 2x6 to spread the load. The pan will take the load, but only if it's spread out pretty good. Autotranny same way. Stick might need a block under the tranny depending on just what type jack and where you have to put it.<P>The only thing that might get in the way is the exhaust. You might need to unbolt the pipe from the manifold to the cat in some cases. If you need to pull up on the pipe more than about an inch, inch and a half, then you'll likely want to unbolt it to prevent damaging the pipe or the exhaust hangers.||||reg||1
Z||000003||JetroGT||04-06-2000||04:34 AM||||Thanks for all of the info! You guys are great. Now I'll order some new mounts and save my self a ton of money on labor! ||||reg||14
